---

<SEOArticle
  title="3-Day Full-Body Workout Plan for Strength"
  description="Follow a simple 3-day full-body workout plan built around squats, hinges, pushes, pulls and carries, with progression and recovery guidance."
  canonical="/training/3-day-full-body-workout-plan/"
  eyebrow="Strength Training"
  heading="A 3-day full-body workout plan for steady strength progress."
  lead="Three full-body sessions per week can be a practical middle ground: enough training to make progress, with enough space between workouts to recover and stay consistent."
  {faqs}
  {toc}
  secondaryLabel="Dumbbell-Only Plan"
  secondaryHref="/training/dumbbell-only-workout-plan/"
>
  <p><strong>A good three-day plan does not need three completely different workouts.</strong> The goal is to repeat the major movement patterns often enough to improve while varying exercises or emphasis enough to manage fatigue.</p>

  <h2 id="schedule">Example 3-day full-body schedule</h2>
  <div class="grid three">
    <div class="card"><span class="pill">Monday</span><h3>Workout A</h3><p>Squat emphasis, horizontal push and pull, hip hinge and carry.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill coral">Wednesday</span><h3>Workout B</h3><p>Single-leg emphasis, overhead push, vertical or angled pull, glutes and trunk.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="pill orange">Friday</span><h3>Workout C</h3><p>Moderate full-body work with a different squat, press and row variation.</p></div>
  </div>

  <p>Tuesday, Thursday and the weekend can be used for rest, walking, mobility, recreational activity or easier conditioning depending on your goals.</p>

  <h2 id="workouts">The three workouts</h2>

  <h3>Workout A</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Goblet squat or back squat: 3 sets of 6 to 10</li>
    <li>Bench press or dumbbell floor press: 3 sets of 6 to 10</li>
    <li>One-arm dumbbell row or cable row: 3 sets of 8 to 12</li>
    <li>Romanian deadlift: 2 to 3 sets of 8 to 10</li>
    <li>Farmer carry: 3 short carries</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Workout B</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Reverse lunge or split squat: 3 sets of 6 to 10 per side</li>
    <li>Standing dumbbell press: 3 sets of 6 to 10</li>
    <li>Lat pulldown or assisted pull-up: 3 sets of 8 to 12</li>
    <li>Hip bridge or hip thrust: 3 sets of 8 to 15</li>
    <li>Side plank or suitcase carry: 3 rounds</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Workout C</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Front squat, leg press or goblet squat: 3 sets of 8 to 12</li>
    <li>Incline dumbbell press or push-up: 3 sets of 8 to 12</li>
    <li>Chest-supported row: 3 sets of 8 to 12</li>
    <li>Dumbbell deadlift or kettlebell hinge: 2 to 3 sets of 8 to 12</li>
    <li>Optional curls, triceps or calves: 2 sets each</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Do not confuse variety with progress.</strong> Keep the main exercises in place for several weeks so you can compare performance from session to session.</div>

  <h2 id="progression">How to progress the plan</h2>
  <p>Use a load that allows stable technique through the target range. When you can complete all prescribed sets near the top of the range with a few repetitions still possible, make a small increase in load or difficulty.</p>

  <p>A simple progression sequence is:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Learn the movement and establish a repeatable starting load.</li>
    <li>Add repetitions until you reach the top of the target range.</li>
    <li>Increase the load slightly.</li>
    <li>Return to the lower end of the repetition range.</li>
    <li>Repeat while technique and recovery remain stable.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How hard should the sets feel?</h2>
  <p>Most working sets do not need to reach complete muscular failure. Especially for beginners, stopping with a few good repetitions left can make technique easier to maintain and recovery more predictable.</p>

  <p>If performance drops sharply across several workouts, sleep is poor, soreness is lingering or motivation is consistently low, consider reducing volume before adding more exercises.</p>

  <h2 id="recovery">Recovery between full-body workouts</h2>
  <p>A three-day schedule works partly because there is usually a lower-stress day between lifting sessions. Recovery still depends on sleep, nutrition, total life stress and the amount of other activity you do.</p>

  <p>Use easy movement when it helps you feel better. A short <a href="/recovery/10-minute-mobility-routine/">mobility routine</a> or walk can fit well on non-lifting days without turning recovery into another demanding session.</p>

  <h2>When two days may be better</h2>
  <p>If your schedule is unpredictable, you are brand new to lifting or three workouts repeatedly get skipped, a two-day plan may be the better program. Consistency matters more than choosing the theoretically perfect weekly split. Our <a href="/training/beginner-strength-training-program/">beginner strength training program</a> is built around that simpler starting point.</p>
